      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\) and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\), we will consider the constraints and count the valid numbers accordingly.\n\n### Step 1: Counting the number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\)\n\nA four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of the odd digits: 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. \n\nFirst, we count the total number of four-digit numbers:\n\\[ 9999 - 1000 + 1 = 9000 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of four-digit numbers with a specific last digit:\n- If the last digit is 1, the first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\n- If the last digit is 3, the first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\n- If the last digit is 5, the first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\n- If the last digit is 7, the first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\n- If the last digit is 9, the first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\nSince there are 5 choices for the last digit, we multiply the number of choices for the first three digits by 5:\n\\[ 5 \\times 100 = 500 \\]\n\nThus, the number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\) is:\n\\[ A = 500 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Counting the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\)\n\nA four-digit multiple of 5 must have a last digit that is 0 or 5.\n\nFirst, we count the total number of four-digit numbers:\n\\[ 9999 - 1000 + 1 = 9000 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of four-digit numbers with a last digit of 0:\n- The first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of four-digit numbers with a last digit of 5:\n- The first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 (10 choices) and 0 to 9 (10 choices):\n  \\[ 10 \\times 10 = 100 \\]\n\nSince there are 2 choices for the last digit (0 or 5), we add the two counts:\n\\[ 100 + 100 = 200 \\]\n\nThus, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\) is:\n\\[ B = 200 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculating \\( A + B \\)\n\nFinally, we sum the number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\) and the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\):\n\\[ A + B = 500 + 200 = 700 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{700}\n\\]",
