      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we'll calculate the values of \\(A\\) (the number of four-digit odd numbers) and \\(B\\) (the number of four-digit multiples of 5) separately, and then find their sum \\(A + B\\).\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(A\\)\n\nA four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\}. Therefore, we need to count the number of possible choices for each digit while ensuring the last digit is odd.\n\n1. The first digit (thousands place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices).\n2. The second digit (hundreds place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n3. The third digit (tens place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n4. The last digit (units place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices).\n\nThe total number of four-digit odd numbers \\(A\\) is calculated as follows:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 9 = 8100\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(B\\)\n\nA four-digit number that is a multiple of 5 must end in either 0 or 5. We will count the number of such possibilities for the last digit and the first three digits.\n\n1. The last digit can be either 0 or 5 (2 choices).\n2. The first digit (thousands place) can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices).\n3. The second digit (hundreds place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n4. The third digit (tens place) can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n\nThe total number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\(B\\) is calculated as follows:\n\\[\nB = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 1800\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(A + B\\)\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\):\n\\[\nA + B = 8100 + 1800 = 9900\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9900}\n\\]",
