      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the values of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we will consider the properties of four-digit odd numbers and multiples of 5 separately.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the number of four-digit odd numbers, \\(A\\).**\n\nA four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\}. We can count these numbers by considering each possible last digit and the corresponding thousands, hundreds, and tens digits.\n\nThe thousands, hundreds, and tens digits can each be any digit from 1 to 9 (since a four-digit number cannot start with 0). The units digit (the last digit) can be any digit from 1 to 9. Therefore, for each choice of the last digit, there are \\(9 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 900\\) possible four-digit numbers.\n\nSince there are 5 possible choices for the last digit (1, 3, 5, 7, 9), the total number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n\\[\n5 \\times 900 = 4500\n\\]\nThus, \\(A = 4500\\).\n\n**Step 2: Determine the number of four-digit multiples of 5, \\(B\\).**\n\nA number is a multiple of 5 if its last digit is either 0 or 5. We will count the number of four-digit numbers ending in 0 and the number of four-digit numbers ending in 5 separately.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of four-digit numbers ending in 0} = 900 \\quad (\\text{same as the number of three-digit numbers ending in 0})\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Number of four-digit numbers ending in 5} = 900 \\quad (\\text{same as the number of three-digit numbers ending in 5})\n\\]\n\nAdding these together gives the total number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\n900 + 900 = 1800\n\\]\nThus, \\(B = 1800\\).\n\n**Step 3: Find \\(A + B\\).**\n\nFinally, we add the number of four-digit odd numbers and the number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1800 = 6300\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6300}\n\\]",
